<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All About the Weather – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> A Perfect Day!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Reading: Find specific information in a text (e.g., names, quantities, dates). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify simple story elements, such as characters or settings, in short texts about the weather (e.g., recognizing "the boy" and "the rainy day" in a story). Can find specific weather-related information in a text (e.g., locating "rain" or "sunny" when looking for weather descriptions or identifying the number of items, such as "two umbrellas"). Can identify the initial and final phonemes of spoken weather-related words and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning and end of words with visual support (e.g., identifying the /w/ and /i/ sounds in "windy" when shown an image of wind).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of weather vocabulary (e.g., 'sunny day', 'rainy day', 'windy day', 'cloudy day') introduced in Lesson 1. Students look at the flashcards and call out the weather. The teacher asks, "What's the weather like today?" and models the answer, "It's a sunny day!" while pointing to the sun. Students repeat chorally. The teacher then shows clothing flashcards (e.g., 'umbrella', 'coat', 'hat') and asks, "What do you wear on a rainy day?" encouraging students to point and say the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a big picture showing a 'sunny day' and the word "sunny" written below it. The teacher points to the word "sunny" and clearly pronounces /s/ for "sunny" and /r/ for "rainy" using corresponding flashcards. For phonemic awareness, the teacher shows a picture of wind and the word "windy". The teacher says "windy" aloud, exaggerating the /w/ sound at the beginning and the /i/ sound at the end. The teacher then points to the letter 'W' and 'Y' in "windy", saying "W for /w/, Y for /i/." Students imitate the sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a rainy day or a sunny day?" (Showing a picture of a sunny day). Expected response: "Sunny day!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we wear a coat on a sunny day?" (Teacher shakes head 'no'). Expected response: "No!" (or "No, a hat!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> A large illustrated storybook page or projected slide with a very short narrative excerpt (2-3 sentences) titled "My Perfect Day!" This text uses simple present tense, adjectives + nouns, and target vocabulary. Example text: "This is Tom. He likes sunny days. It's a sunny day today! Tom plays outside. He wears a hat. This is Mia. She likes rainy days. It's a rainy day today. Mia plays inside. She wears a yellow coat and boots." The text will be accompanied by clear pictures of Tom and Mia in their respective weather scenarios and clothing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to identify simple story elements (characters, setting) and specific information in a short text about weather and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud slowly, pointing to each word as it is read, and to the corresponding pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Who is in the story?" (Students point to Tom and Mia). "What is the weather like in Tom's part of the story?" (Students point to the sunny picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads sentences from the text, then makes a statement. Students show "thumbs up" for true or "thumbs down" for false. E.g., Teacher reads, "He likes sunny days." Then says, "Tom likes rainy days." (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher asks, "Who wears a coat?" Students point to Mia in the picture. The teacher asks, "What does Tom wear?" Students point to the hat.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple sentences with adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day"), identifying characters and settings, and locating specific vocabulary words (e.g., "hat", "coat", "umbrella").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Read and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences from the story, each accompanied by two picture options. Example: "Tom likes _______ days." (Picture of sun / Picture of rain). Students read the sentence and circle the correct picture. Another exercise involves reading sentences and circling the correct vocabulary word (e.g., "Mia wears a _______. (coat / hat)"). This reinforces reading comprehension and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Read and Find):</b> Students receive a simplified version of the "My Perfect Day!" story (3-4 sentences) with accompanying illustrations. The teacher instructs students to read the text silently. Then, the teacher calls out a weather word (e.g., "sunny", "rainy") or a clothing item (e.g., "umbrella", "boots"), and students must find and underline that word in their text. The teacher also asks "What is the weather like?" or "Who likes sunny days?" and students point to or underline the answer in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Read and Match):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (showing sunny, rainy, windy days, and people wearing different clothes) and a set of simple sentence strips (e.g., "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." "It's rainy. I need my umbrella."). Students take turns reading a sentence strip and matching it to the correct picture card. They check each other's answers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task Format:</b> "Weather Reader and Drawer" activity. Students will demonstrate their reading comprehension by interpreting short descriptions and translating them into visual form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a worksheet containing two blank frames and two simple written descriptions of "A Perfect Day!" for different characters.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example Descriptions (Pre-A1.4 level):
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Frame 1:</b> "This is Leo. It is a sunny day. He plays outside. Leo wears a hat."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Frame 2:</b> "This is Sofia. It is a rainy day. She plays inside. Sofia needs her umbrella and coat."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each description independently. After reading, they draw a picture in the corresponding frame that accurately reflects the weather, character, activity, and clothing described.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing students as they read and draw, providing support as needed. This activity emphasizes reading for specific information and visualizing the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "Read and Match" activity on a mini-worksheet. They read 3-4 simple sentences about weather and clothing, then draw lines to match each sentence to the correct picture (from a set of 4-5 pictures). This includes identifying initial and final phonemes for "windy".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of matching:</b> Matches sentences to correct pictures (e.g., "It's a sunny day." matches to a sun picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identification of specific information:</b> Correctly identifies characters (e.g., "Leo") and objects (e.g., "umbrella", "coat") from text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Correctly identifies the initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds in "windy" when prompted visually (e.g., by circling the letters 'W' and 'Y' in the word "windy" presented with a picture).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to complete the following: "Circle how you feel about reading about the weather today: Happy / Okay / A little hard." And "I can find the word '_______' (e.g., 'sunny' or 'rainy') in a story. Yes / No."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of reading about the weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read and understand?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b> The reading activities for identifying story elements and specific information worked well with visual support. Students engaged well with circling and matching tasks. Some students still need more practice recognizing the initial and final phonemes for "windy" despite the visual and auditory modeling; I will integrate more explicit phonemic awareness drills using flashcards and letter tracing in the next lesson's warm-up. The simple sentence structures and repeated vocabulary were appropriate for the Pre-A1.4 level. I need to ensure all students have clear access to the texts and visuals for optimal reading comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the weather vocabulary and simple sentences they practiced reading today to describe weather and talk about their preferences in a communicative, interactive way.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>